      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that there exists some \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = -2 \\). This means we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\]\n\nRearranging the terms, we get:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation of the form \\( Ax^2 + Bx + C = 0 \\), where \\( A = 1 \\), \\( B = 3 \\), and \\( C = c + 2 \\). For \\( x \\) to have real solutions, the discriminant of this quadratic equation must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( Ax^2 + Bx + C = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = B^2 - 4AC \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( A = 1 \\), \\( B = 3 \\), and \\( C = c + 2 \\) into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8 \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 1 - 4c \\]\n\nFor \\( x \\) to have real solutions, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[ 1 - 4c \\geq 0 \\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[ 1 \\geq 4c \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\geq c \\]\n\\[ c \\leq \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}} \\]",
